> The quickest and surest way to convert is to sacrifice a unicorn of
> your (current) alignment on another alignment's altar.
[...]

Thanks. That answers the main question quite well.

> There are some side effects.

> 1) You lose any protection immediately, and have to buy it back.

Thanks for the reminder.

> 2) You experience divine anger.  It will take some sacrificing to
> mollify that, and if you're converting to chaotic it's even harder.

Ok.

> If I was converting a lawful character to a chaotic alignment, the
> simplest thing to do would be to find a chaotic altar and reverse
> genocide white unicorns. [...]

That's the thought that came to me above when you mentioned them. :)

(Nice side effect about the mollyfying gift, didn't know, or remember,  
that.)

> There is *another* method as well... I don't remember for certain,
> but I think if you manage to push your alignment down into the deep
> negative (consistently healing creatures if you're chaotic, killing
> peacefuls if you're lawful), there's a chance an altar sacrifice on a
> foreign altar will convert *you* instead of the altar. [...]

I think I've tried that once, and kept having to re-align the altar to  
chaotic because I was never bad enough for the god. :)

> I have ascended two converted characters of the opposite alignment
> they started with (after the quest, natch). I recommend it for
> anyone .. it's quite fun.

Indeed. :) I know my male valk was chaotic, too.

(Checking, there was also a lawful elf priest in Slash'EM, but it may be  
that elves are lawful there anyway, and drows are the chaotic version.)

